{# ONE ROW OF THE NAV, AT ANY DEPTH — and its branch, if it has one.

   Recursive on purpose: the source decides the nesting and the shell renders
   whatever nesting it is given, because how deep a location tree goes is not
   something a layout can know. Three treatments, by depth:

     1. a section's own row     — icon + label, the collapsed rail's only survivor
     2. a place inside it       — the emphasised row the branch hangs from
     3. and below               — the rows of the place you are in

   A ROW WITH NOWHERE TO GO RENDERS INERT rather than vanishing. The host needs
   this — a surface whose route has not merged yet — and the honest treatment is
   the one the design settled: visible, dimmed, not a link, carrying its own
   explanation in a title. The product says "this is coming" instead of
   pretending it was never planned.

   FIVE TREATMENTS, BY DEPTH — the design distinguishes them and so must the
   shell: a section row (1), the place the branch hangs from (2), that place's
   own sibling screens (3, `ntt`, with `par` on the one that carries modules),
   the module rows under it (4, `ntm`, each with its identity dot, and `par`
   while one of its own screens is lit), and a module's own data places under
   that (5, `nts`). The fifth rung carries NO marker of its own: the module's
   square dot one level up is the identity, and repeating a glyph below it would
   read as a second kind of thing.

   TWO MARKINGS, AND THE PATH DECIDES WHICH — ruled 2026-09-20, one ground per
   tree. `on` is the row the viewer is on: the accent, the accent 12% ground and
   the left focus line, spent once per sidebar however deep the branch runs.
   `path` is a row that row hangs off — a section above a screen, an area above
   its tabs, Modules above a module — and it carries the accent as INK only: no
   ground, no second left line. In the owner's words, marking parent and child
   with a background both is a bit too much. Neither class comes from a source:
   the shell derives both from where the viewer is, which is the only way every
   page's tree can read the same. `par` stays what it always was — the affordance
   of a row that has a branch — and says nothing about location. #}
{%- set _tag = item.url is null ? 'span' : 'a' -%}
{%- set _kids = item.children|length > 0 -%}
{%- set _row = depth == 1 ? 'nav-item' : (depth == 2 ? 'nta' : (depth == 3 ? 'ntt' : (depth == 4 ? 'ntm' : 'nts'))) -%}
{%- set _group = depth == 1 ? 'ntree' : (depth == 2 ? 'nta-group' : 'ntgroup') -%}

<{{ _tag }} class="{{ _row }}{{ depth >= 3 and _kids ? ' par' }}{{ item.current ? ' on' }}{{ item.path ? ' path' }}{{ item.url is null ? ' off' }}{{ _kids and not item.open ? ' closed' }}"
    {%- if _kids %} data-nav-key="{{ item.key }}"{% endif %}
    {%- if item.url is not null %} href="{{ item.url }}"{% endif %}
    {%- if item.hint is not null %} title="{{ item.hint }}"{% endif %}>
    {%- if item.icon is not null %}{{ ux_icon(item.icon) }}{% endif -%}
    {%- if depth == 1 %}<span>{{ item.label }}</span>
    {%- elseif depth == 2 %}<b>{{ item.label }}</b>
    {%- elseif depth == 3 %}{{ item.label }}
    {%- elseif depth == 4 %}<i class="mdot{{ item.tone is not null ? ' ' ~ item.tone }}"
        {%- if item.swatch is not null %} style="background:{{ item.swatch }}"{% endif %}></i>{{ item.label }}
    {%- else %}{{ item.label }}{% endif -%}
    {%- if _kids %}<button class="chev" type="button" aria-label="Fold {{ item.label }}"
            data-action="uhifadhi--shell-bundle--sidebar-tree#fold">{{ ux_icon('shell:chevron-down') }}</button>{% endif -%}
</{{ _tag }}>

{%- if _kids %}
    <div class="{{ _group }}{{ item.open ? '' : ' closed' }}">
        {%- for child in item.children -%}
            {#- A ROW WHOSE CHILDREN ARE ITS OWN SCREENS skips the place rung:
                there is no place between a section and its screens, and drawing
                one would give a reader two kinds of row for one kind of thing. -#}
            {{ include('@Shell/_nav_item.html.twig', {item: child, depth: depth + (item.screens ? 2 : 1)}, with_context: false) }}
        {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
{% endif -%}
